mh2lm: limit key IRQ channel register probes to the IRQ-owned EE0 APID (0132)

The 0114a5 pure-map diagnostic reaches iOS after mapping all three APIDs
for PON PPID 0x8. The earlier 0114a3 diagnostic returned to the LG BSP
when it also read ACC_ENABLE. Runtime metadata shows APIDs 1 and 19 are
owned by EEs 1 and 4, while APID 248 belongs to EE0 and has the IRQ-owner
bit. Linux's PMIC-arb v5 driver chooses the EE-owned APID for a duplicate
PPID and only probes IRQ status for APIDs whose irq_ee matches the current
EE. Avoid channel register accesses on the other EEs' APIDs here.

Keep metadata and owner-only diagnostics unchanged. For any diagnostic
that maps or reads a channel, log skipped duplicates and continue to the
EE0 IRQ-owned APID. This remains read-only and does not register GIC,
enable, or clear an interrupt. It retains the existing key polling path.

--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
@@ -94,6 +94,17 @@ sp_irq_diag_peripheral(unsigned sid, unsigned peripheral, const char *name,
 		if ((parts & SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_OWNER) != 0) {
 			ownerMask = sp_rd(intr, 4u * ownerWord);
 		}
+		/* v5 can have duplicate PPIDs for different execution environments.
+		 * The channel's IRQ registers are only probed through our own EE0,
+		 * IRQ-owned APID; do not touch a foreign EE's channel registers. */
+		if ((parts & (SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_CHANNEL |
+		    SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_ENABLE |
+		    SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_STATUS)) != 0 &&
+		    (owner != 0u || (map & SP_IRQ_OWNER_BIT) == 0)) {
+			printf("SWEET SPMI IRQ: %s ppid=%#x apid=%u channel skipped owner=%u irq-owner=%u (EE0 IRQ owner required)\n",
+			    name, ppid, apid, owner, (map & SP_IRQ_OWNER_BIT) ? 1 : 0);
+			continue;
+		}
 		if ((parts & (SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_CHANNEL |
 		    SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_ENABLE |
 		    SWEET_SPMI_IRQ_DIAG_ROUTE_STATUS)) != 0) {
